<LearningGuideLayout
  title="How to Trigger Gameplay from Blender Animation Frames"
  description="Place FPE frame events in Blender animations to trigger sounds, impacts, cameras, dialogue, and level commands at exact moments in Godot."
  summary="The animator already knows the exact frame when a foot lands, a latch clicks, or a magical puff should appear. Put the event on that authored moment in Blender and let the exported animation carry its timing into the game."
  relatedSlugs={[
    "make-doors-switches-in-blender",
    "create-triggers-in-blender-for-godot",
    "blender-to-godot-gameplay-import",
  ]}
  godotSource={{
    label: "Animation track types",
    href: "https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/animation/animation_track_types.html",
  }}
  fpeHeading="What FPE creates in Godot"
>
  <div class="guide-summary">
    <strong>You will make:</strong> a Blender action with a named FPE Frame Event
    on the exact beat where gameplay should respond.
  </div>
  <h2>When should you use frame events?</h2><p>
    Use them when timing belongs to the authored animation: footsteps, impact
    windows, particles, audio cues, prop release, or cutscene beats. Use an
    animation-finished signal for end-of-clip state changes. Do not use frame
    events to update a value continuously; property tracks already do that
    better.
  </p>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/door-action-timeline.png"
    alt="Blender Action Editor showing a door animation and the playhead at frame 42"
    caption="Work in the Action Editor so the event can be placed against a precise moment in the animation."
  />
  <h2>Place the event in Blender</h2><ol>
    <li>
      Open the action in the <strong>Dope Sheet → Action Editor</strong>.
    </li><li>
      In <strong>Folded Paper Engine (Animation)</strong>, choose whether the
      action should autoplay or loop.
    </li><li>Move the playhead to the exact authored moment.</li><li>
      In <strong>Folded Paper Engine (Frame Events)</strong>, add a Frame Event
      and give it a stable Scene Event name such as <code>FOOTSTEP</code> or <code
        >DOOR_LATCHED</code
      >.
    </li><li>
      Create the matching Scene Event and start with one command you can see or
      hear.
    </li><li>
      Export with animation and Custom Properties enabled, then test normal,
      interrupted, and looping playback in Godot.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/animation-settings-panel.png"
    alt="FPE Animation panel open alongside the Blender Action Editor"
    caption="The Animation panel controls how the selected action is exposed to the exported scene."
  />
  <h2>The traditional Godot workflow</h2><ol>
    <li>Select the animation in <code>AnimationPlayer</code>.</li><li>
      Add a <strong>Call Method Track</strong> targeting a stable relay node.
    </li><li>
      Insert a key at the exact moment and call a method such as <code
        >animation_event(&amp;"FOOTSTEP")</code
      >.
    </li><li>
      Have that relay emit a signal or dispatch the named event to the owning
      character or scene.
    </li><li>Test forward, looping, blended, and interrupted playback.</li>
  </ol>
  <pre><code>signal frame_event(event_id: StringName)

func animation_event(event_id: StringName) -&gt; void:
    frame_event.emit(event_id)</code></pre>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/frame-event-panel.png"
    alt="FPE Frame Events panel with DOOR_LATCHED at frame 42"
    caption="A named frame event marks the gameplay moment without putting Godot implementation details into the animation."
  />
  <h2>Why use a relay?</h2><p>
    A method track can call any method, but directly calling <code
      >delete_enemy()</code
    > or <code>load_next_level()</code> makes the animation difficult to reuse. Calling
    one stable relay with a named event keeps the clip portable. It also gives you
    one place to log, ignore stale events, or translate authored names into gameplay
    commands.
  </p>
  <h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Expecting preview calls:</strong> Godot does not execute method-track
      calls while previewing in the editor.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Destructive immediate callbacks:</strong> deferred method-call mode
      is safer when events modify the scene tree or animation player.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Polling the current frame:</strong> fast playback and frame-rate changes
      can skip equality checks.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Forgetting interruption:</strong> decide whether cancelling an attack
      also clears its pending hit window.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Fragment slot="fpe"
    ><p>
      FPE carries the authored timing through glTF, attaches a <code
        >FrameEventRunner</code
      > when the level loads, and dispatches the matching Scene Event during playback.
      The same command catalog used by triggers can play audio, switch cameras, start
      conversations, load levels, or call custom event-driven code.
    </p><p>
      <a href="/docs/fpe-animation-and-events.html"
        >Read the animation and frame events reference</a
      >.
    </p></Fragment
  >
</LearningGuideLayout>
